      {
        "id": "story",
        "type": "story",
        "provenance": "original",
        "title": {
          "en": "Heads or tails?"
        },
        "text": {
          "en": "Before a cricket match the captains toss a coin. Each side has the same chance. Probability measures how likely an event is on a scale of 0 to 1."
        }
      },
      {
        "id": "explain",
        "type": "explanation",
        "provenance": "original",
        "keepEnglishTerms": [
          "0 <= P(E) <= 1",
          "P(E) = favourable / total",
          "cards",
          "complementary events",
          "die example",
          "experiment and event",
          "sum = 1",
          "sure = 1, impossible = 0"
        ],
        "text": {
          "en": "An experiment such as tossing a coin or rolling a die has outcomes. An event is a collection of outcomes.\nProbability of an event E = number of outcomes favourable to E divided by the number of all possible outcomes, when outcomes are equally likely.\nFor a fair die, P(getting 4) = 1/6 and P(even number) = 3/6 = 1/2.\nAn event that is sure to happen has probability 1. An impossible event has probability 0.\nSo the probability of any event lies between 0 and 1.\nThe probabilities of all elementary events of an experiment add up to 1.\nP(E) + P(not E) = 1. The event not E is the complement of E.\nFor a deck of 52 cards, P(red card) = 26/52 = 1/2. P(ace of spades) = 1/52."
        }
      },
